{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_0","type":"agent_message","text":"{\"id\":\"R-1:2\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"The sequence moves from a mercy-named invocation to praise of Allah as Lord of all worlds, so the mercy names frame the surah’s first declaration of praise and universal lordship.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"1:1\",\"ar\":\"ٱلرَّحْمَٰنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ\"},{\"ref\":\"1:2\",\"ar\":\"ٱلْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ رَبِّ ٱلْعَٰلَمِينَ\"}],\"activation\":\"Both verses name Allah, and praise follows the invocation immediately.\",\"limits\":\"The sequence gives no explicit causal link saying that praise follows because of mercy.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-1:3\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"The pair ٱلرَّحْمَٰنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ recurs as its own verse after praise of the Lord of all worlds, giving the focus epithets independent structural weight in the surah.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"1:1\",\"ar\":\"ٱلرَّحْمَٰنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ\"},{\"ref\":\"1:3\",\"ar\":\"ٱلرَّحْمَٰنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ\"}],\"activation\":\"The exact pair returns after 1:2, in a separate ayah.\",\"limits\":\"Verse 1:3 does not name Allah explicitly, so its referent is carried by the surrounding passage.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-1:4\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"The mercy pair is followed by مَٰلِكِ يَوْمِ ٱلدِّينِ, placing judgment within the opening portrait that also names mercy.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"1:3\",\"ar\":\"ٱلرَّحْمَٰنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ\"},{\"ref\":\"1:4\",\"ar\":\"مَٰلِكِ يَوْمِ ٱلدِّينِ\"}],\"activation\":\"The titles occur in immediate sequence before the prayer turns to worship and help-seeking.\",\"limits\":\"There is no adversative or explanation specifying how mercy and judgment relate, or what judgment’s outcome will be.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-1:5\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"After naming Allah and divine mercy, the prayer shifts to direct, exclusive address: إِيَّاكَ نَعْبُدُ وَإِيَّاكَ نَسْتَعِينُ. This makes worship and reliance the response within the opening portrait.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"1:1\",\"ar\":\"بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرَّحْمَٰنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ\"},{\"ref\":\"1:5\",\"ar\":\"إِيَّاكَ نَعْبُدُ وَإِيَّاكَ نَسْتَعِينُ\"}],\"activation\":\"The second-person address follows the divine titles, praise, lordship, and judgment.\",\"limits\":\"The sequence does not state that mercy alone causes worship or seeking help.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-1:6\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"The request ٱهْدِنَا arrives after worship and seeking help, making the opening name and mercy pair part of the frame for a prayer that asks for guidance.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"1:1\",\"ar\":\"بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرَّحْمَٰنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ\"},{\"ref\":\"1:5\",\"ar\":\"وَإِيَّاكَ نَسْتَعِينُ\"},{\"ref\":\"1:6\",\"ar\":\"ٱهْدِنَا ٱلصِّرَٰطَ ٱلْمُسْتَقِيمَ\"}],\"activation\":\"The first request follows the declaration of exclusive worship and reliance.\",\"limits\":\"No word in 1:6 explicitly identifies guidance as an act of mercy.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-1:7\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"The closing verse defines the requested path by favor received and by the boundaries of anger and straying, giving the mercy-named opening a destination marked by guidance and its dangers.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"1:1\",\"ar\":\"ٱلرَّحْمَٰنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ\"},{\"ref\":\"1:6\",\"ar\":\"ٱهْدِنَا ٱلصِّرَٰطَ ٱلْمُسْتَقِيمَ\"},{\"ref\":\"1:7\",\"ar\":\"صِرَٰطَ ٱلَّذِينَ أَنْعَمْتَ عَلَيْهِمْ غَيْرِ ٱلْمَغْضُوبِ عَلَيْهِمْ وَلَا ٱلضَّآلِّينَ\"}],\"activation\":\"The final verse answers the preceding request for the straight path.\",\"limits\":\"Verse 1:7 does not repeat the mercy root or explicitly equate favor with mercy.\",\"support\":\"medium\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-2:37\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"The al-Rahim epithet closes an episode where Adam receives words and God accepts his repentance, giving the focus epithet a clear Quranic setting of return received.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"2:37\",\"ar\":\"فَتَلَقَّىٰٓ ءَادَمُ مِن رَّبِّهِۦ كَلِمَٰتٍۢ فَتَابَ عَلَيْهِ\"},{\"ref\":\"2:37\",\"ar\":\"إِنَّهُۥ هُوَ ٱلتَّوَّابُ ٱلرَّحِيمُ\"}],\"activation\":\"ٱلرَّحِيمُ is paired with ٱلتَّوَّابُ after the explicit acceptance of Adam’s repentance.\",\"limits\":\"This episode does not define every use of ٱلرَّحِيمُ as a response to repentance.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-2:54\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"The same ٱلتَّوَّابُ ٱلرَّحِيمُ ending follows a command to repent and a severe response to calf worship; the pair is voiced after a grave breach and God’s turning back to the people.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"2:54\",\"ar\":\"فَتُوبُوٓا۟ إِلَىٰ بَارِئِكُمْ فَٱقْتُلُوٓا۟ أَنفُسَكُمْ\"},{\"ref\":\"2:54\",\"ar\":\"فَتَابَ عَلَيْكُمْ ۚ إِنَّهُۥ هُوَ ٱلتَّوَّابُ ٱلرَّحِيمُ\"}],\"activation\":\"The verse explicitly says فَتَابَ عَلَيْكُمْ before naming God ٱلتَّوَّابُ ٱلرَّحِيمُ.\",\"limits\":\"The wording does not say that the severe command itself is an act of mercy.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-2:64\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"After the people turn away from the covenant, 2:64 sets their possible loss against Allah’s favor and mercy. This gives the rahma root a saving force in a setting of failure.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"2:64\",\"ar\":\"ثُمَّ تَوَلَّيْتُم مِّنۢ بَعْدِ ذَٰلِكَ\"},{\"ref\":\"2:64\",\"ar\":\"فَلَوْلَا فَضْلُ ٱللَّهِ عَلَيْكُمْ وَرَحْمَتُهُۥ لَكُنتُم مِّنَ ٱلْخَٰسِرِينَ\"}],\"activation\":\"The counterfactual links the people’s turning away, divine favor and mercy, and the prospect of loss.\",\"limits\":\"This noun use of رَحْمَة does not establish that every use of the focus epithets has this specific covenant setting.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-2:105\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"In a dispute over revelation, Allah is said to grant His mercy to whom He wills. This places the rahma root in a context of divine choice and recipients, while the focus names themselves specify no recipients.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"2:105\",\"ar\":\"وَٱللَّهُ يَخْتَصُّ بِرَحْمَتِهِۦ مَن يَشَآءُ\"},{\"ref\":\"2:105\",\"ar\":\"وَٱللَّهُ ذُو ٱلْفَضْلِ ٱلْعَظِيمِ\"}],\"activation\":\"The shared rahma root appears in a verse about whether good will be sent to the believers.\",\"limits\":\"This verse does not specify whether the focus names imply any particular distribution of mercy.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-2:128\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"Ibrahim and Ismail’s prayer asks for acceptance, submission, and knowledge of the rites, then asks God to turn toward them and closes with ٱلتَّوَّابُ ٱلرَّحِيمُ. The pair thus also occurs at the close of a communal supplication.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"2:127\",\"ar\":\"رَبَّنَا تَقَبَّلْ مِنَّآ\"},{\"ref\":\"2:128\",\"ar\":\"وَتُبْ عَلَيْنَآ\"},{\"ref\":\"2:128\",\"ar\":\"إِنَّكَ أَنتَ ٱلتَّوَّابُ ٱلرَّحِيمُ\"}],\"activation\":\"The prayer directly addresses God and places repentance and the mercy epithet beside requests about worship and its rites.\",\"limits\":\"The passage does not say that the rites themselves are a condition for receiving mercy.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-2:143\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"At the qibla change, the assurance that Allah will not let the believers’ faith go to waste ends with رَءُوفٌ رَّحِيمٌ. The Rahim name therefore appears in a passage where divine guidance is tested and reassurance is given.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"2:143\",\"ar\":\"وَإِن كَانَتْ لَكَبِيرَةً إِلَّا عَلَى ٱلَّذِينَ هَدَى ٱللَّهُ\"},{\"ref\":\"2:143\",\"ar\":\"وَمَا كَانَ ٱللَّهُ لِيُضِيعَ إِيمَٰنَكُمْ\"},{\"ref\":\"2:143\",\"ar\":\"إِنَّ ٱللَّهَ بِٱلنَّاسِ لَرَءُوفٌۭ رَّحِيمٌۭ\"},{\"ref\":\"1:6\",\"ar\":\"ٱهْدِنَا ٱلصِّرَٰطَ ٱلْمُسْتَقِيمَ\"}],\"activation\":\"The Fatiha’s request for guidance and 2:143’s account of guidance and a tested direction meet around the Rahim epithet.\",\"limits\":\"This does not equate the qibla test with the straight path requested in the Fatiha.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-2:160\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"Those who repent, repair, and clarify after concealing revelation are exempted from the preceding curse; God says He turns to them and names Himself ٱلتَّوَّابُ ٱلرَّحِيمُ. Here return is joined to repair and disclosure.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"2:159\",\"ar\":\"أُو۟لَٰٓئِكَ يَلْعَنُهُمُ ٱللَّهُ\"},{\"ref\":\"2:160\",\"ar\":\"إِلَّا ٱلَّذِينَ تَابُوا۟ وَأَصْلَحُوا۟ وَبَيَّنُوا۟\"},{\"ref\":\"2:160\",\"ar\":\"فَأُو۟لَٰٓئِكَ أَتُوبُ عَلَيْهِمْ ۚ وَأَنَا ٱلتَّوَّابُ ٱلرَّحِيمُ\"}],\"activation\":\"The mercy epithet follows an explicit exception and the actions that qualify the group described there.\",\"limits\":\"This verse concerns this specified group and does not set out a universal account of all repentance.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-2:163\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"Immediately after a verse describing unrelieved punishment, the text declares one God and names Him ٱلرَّحْمَٰنُ ٱلرَّحِيمُ. The exact focus pair appears within a declaration of exclusive divinity after judgment language.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"2:162\",\"ar\":\"لَا يُخَفَّفُ عَنْهُمُ ٱلْعَذَابُ وَلَا هُمْ يُنظَرُونَ\"},{\"ref\":\"2:163\",\"ar\":\"لَّآ إِلَٰهَ إِلَّا هُوَ ٱلرَّحْمَٰنُ ٱلرَّحِيمُ\"},{\"ref\":\"1:1\",\"ar\":\"ٱلرَّحْمَٰنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ\"}],\"activation\":\"The exact pair from the focus verse follows the passage’s transition from punishment to divine oneness.\",\"limits\":\"The adjacent verses do not say that this mercy pair is a remedy for the punished group.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-2:173\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"After dietary prohibitions, a compelled person who does not transgress is declared free of sin, and Allah is named غَفُورٌ رَّحِيمٌ. The Rahim name appears at a legal edge where necessity changes the ruling.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"2:173\",\"ar\":\"فَمَنِ ٱضْطُرَّ غَيْرَ بَاغٍۢ وَلَا عَادٍۢ فَلَآ إِثْمَ عَلَيْهِ\"},{\"ref\":\"2:173\",\"ar\":\"إِنَّ ٱللَّهَ غَفُورٌۭ رَّحِيمٌ\"}],\"activation\":\"The epithet follows the explicit exception for compulsion.\",\"limits\":\"The verse does not call the exception itself rahma or explain the distinct contribution of each epithet.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-2:182\",\"status\":\"note\",\"finding\":\"After a case involving a potentially wrongful bequest, someone who makes peace between the parties bears no sin, and Allah is named غَفُورٌ رَّحِيمٌ. This places the Rahim name beside interpersonal repair.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"2:182\",\"ar\":\"فَمَنْ خَافَ مِن مُّوصٍۢ جَنَفًا أَوْ إِثْمًۭا فَأَصْلَحَ بَيْنَهُمْ فَلَآ إِثْمَ عَلَيْهِ\"},{\"ref\":\"2:182\",\"ar\":\"إِنَّ ٱللَّهَ غَفُورٌۭ رَّحِيمٌۭ\"}],\"activation\":\"The forgiveness and mercy formula closes the instruction about correcting the situation.\",\"limits\":\"The verse does not state that the reformer’s act is itself the cause of divine mercy.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"medium\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-2:192\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"The fighting passage says that if the opponents desist, Allah is غَفُورٌ رَّحِيمٌ. This gives the Rahim name a setting where mercy follows cessation of aggression.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"2:191\",\"ar\":\"فَإِن قَٰتَلُوكُمْ فَٱقْتُلُوهُمْ\"},{\"ref\":\"2:192\",\"ar\":\"فَإِنِ ٱنتَهَوْا۟ فَإِنَّ ٱللَّهَ غَفُورٌۭ رَّحِيمٌۭ\"},{\"ref\":\"2:193\",\"ar\":\"فَإِنِ ٱنتَهَوْا۟ فَلَا عُدْوَٰنَ إِلَّا عَلَى ٱلظَّٰلِمِينَ\"}],\"activation\":\"The condition فَإِنِ ٱنتَهَوْا۟ explicitly ties the epithet formula to the opponents’ desisting.\",\"limits\":\"The condition belongs to this conflict passage and does not define every context for the focus names.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-2:199\",\"status\":\"note\",\"finding\":\"During the pilgrimage sequence, worshippers are told to seek Allah’s forgiveness and the verse closes with غَفُورٌ رَّحِيمٌ. The Rahim name also accompanies a ritual setting of remembrance and return.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"2:198\",\"ar\":\"فَٱذْكُرُوا۟ ٱللَّهَ عِندَ ٱلْمَشْعَرِ ٱلْحَرَامِ\"},{\"ref\":\"2:199\",\"ar\":\"وَٱسْتَغْفِرُوا۟ ٱللَّهَ\"},{\"ref\":\"2:199\",\"ar\":\"إِنَّ ٱللَّهَ غَفُورٌۭ رَّحِيمٌۭ\"}],\"activation\":\"The formula closes the instruction to seek forgiveness in the pilgrimage passage.\",\"limits\":\"The text does not explain whether the pilgrimage scene changes the meaning of the epithet pair.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"medium\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-2:207\",\"status\":\"open\",\"finding\":\"After describing someone who seeks Allah’s pleasure by giving up his life, the verse calls Allah رَءُوفٌۢ بِٱلْعِبَادِ. This distinct compassion epithet may illuminate the focus pair’s wider field of divine care.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"2:207\",\"ar\":\"يَشْرِى نَفْسَهُ ٱبْتِغَآءَ مَرْضَاتِ ٱللَّهِ\"},{\"ref\":\"2:207\",\"ar\":\"وَٱللَّهُ رَءُوفٌۢ بِٱلْعِبَادِ\"},{\"ref\":\"1:1\",\"ar\":\"ٱلرَّحْمَٰنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ\"}],\"missing\":\"The passage uses the root ر أ ف rather than ر ح م; another Quranic usage or a dictionary account establishing the overlap or contrast between these divine epithets could decide how closely they bear on one another.\",\"support\":\"medium\",\"relevance\":\"medium\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-2:259\",\"status\":\"none\",\"code\":\"no-link\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-3:74\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"The rahma noun is described as something Allah grants to whom He wills, alongside His great favor. This makes divine choice in distributing mercy explicit, while the focus names leave its recipients unstated.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"3:74\",\"ar\":\"يَخْتَصُّ بِرَحْمَتِهِۦ مَن يَشَآءُ\"},{\"ref\":\"3:74\",\"ar\":\"وَٱللَّهُ ذُو ٱلْفَضْلِ ٱلْعَظِيمِ\"},{\"ref\":\"1:1\",\"ar\":\"ٱلرَّحْمَٰنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ\"}],\"activation\":\"The same rahma root occurs in an explicit statement about whom Allah grants mercy.\",\"limits\":\"This verse does not establish that the focus epithets themselves mean selective distribution.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-3:129\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"After saying that Allah forgives or punishes whom He wills, the verse calls Him غَفُورٌ رَّحِيمٌ. The Rahim name is thus voiced alongside sovereign choice over forgiveness and punishment.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"3:129\",\"ar\":\"يَغْفِرُ لِمَن يَشَآءُ وَيُعَذِّبُ مَن يَشَآءُ\"},{\"ref\":\"3:129\",\"ar\":\"وَٱللَّهُ غَفُورٌۭ رَّحِيمٌۭ\"},{\"ref\":\"1:1\",\"ar\":\"ٱلرَّحْمَٰنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ\"}],\"activation\":\"The epithets follow the verse’s explicit contrast between forgiveness and punishment at Allah’s will.\",\"limits\":\"The passage does not assign the same recipient to both outcomes or define the focus pair’s full scope.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-3:154\",\"status\":\"open\",\"finding\":\"The Uhud sequence moves from grief and differing inner states to pardon of those who turned away; the pardon is named with غَفُورٌ حَلِيمٌ, not al-Rahim. This may place the focus name beside a distinct forgiveness and forbearance pairing.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"3:154\",\"ar\":\"وَٱللَّهُ عَلِيمٌۢ بِذَاتِ ٱلصُّدُورِ\"},{\"ref\":\"3:155\",\"ar\":\"وَلَقَدْ عَفَا ٱللَّهُ عَنْهُمْ\"},{\"ref\":\"3:155\",\"ar\":\"إِنَّ ٱللَّهَ غَفُورٌ حَلِيمٌۭ\"},{\"ref\":\"1:1\",\"ar\":\"ٱلرَّحْمَٰنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ\"}],\"missing\":\"A Quranic passage pairing or contrasting al-Rahim with al-Halim in a similar pardon scene could establish whether these formulas deliberately overlap or mark different aspects.\",\"support\":\"medium\",\"relevance\":\"medium\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-4:132\",\"status\":\"none\",\"code\":\"no-link\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-5:54\",\"status\":\"none\",\"code\":\"no-link\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-5:88\",\"status\":\"none\",\"code\":\"no-link\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-6:12\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"After identifying Allah as owner of the heavens and earth, the verse says He has prescribed mercy upon Himself and will gather people for Resurrection. This joins mercy to sovereignty and the coming judgment.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"6:12\",\"ar\":\"قُل لِّمَن مَّا فِى ٱلسَّمَٰوَٰتِ وَٱلْأَرْضِ ۖ قُل لِّلَّهِ\"},{\"ref\":\"6:12\",\"ar\":\"كَتَبَ عَلَىٰ نَفْسِهِ ٱلرَّحْمَةَ\"},{\"ref\":\"6:12\",\"ar\":\"لَيَجْمَعَنَّكُمْ إِلَىٰ يَوْمِ ٱلْقِيَٰمَةِ\"}],\"activation\":\"The explicit rahma root follows the answer that everything in the heavens and earth belongs to Allah and precedes the gathering for judgment.\",\"limits\":\"This verse does not say that prescribed mercy cancels or predetermines the judgment that follows.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-6:54\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"When believers come, their Lord is said to have prescribed mercy upon Himself; the verse then describes the one who repents after wrongdoing and reforms as covered by غَفُورٌ رَّحِيمٌ. Mercy is set beside both a divine commitment and a path of return.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"6:54\",\"ar\":\"كَتَبَ رَبُّكُمْ عَلَىٰ نَفْسِهِ ٱلرَّحْمَةَ\"},{\"ref\":\"6:54\",\"ar\":\"ثُمَّ تَابَ مِنۢ بَعْدِهِۦ وَأَصْلَحَ\"},{\"ref\":\"6:54\",\"ar\":\"فَأَنَّهُۥ غَفُورٌۭ رَّحِيمٌۭ\"}],\"activation\":\"The verse itself connects the rahma root with repentance, reform, and the Rahim epithet.\",\"limits\":\"The conditional case of wrongdoing and reform does not exhaust the scope of mercy prescribed upon God.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-6:79\",\"status\":\"none\",\"code\":\"no-link\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-6:118\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"The instruction to eat what has had Allah’s name mentioned over it gives a concrete Quranic setting for the name formula: divine naming is spoken in connection with food and its permissibility.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"1:1\",\"ar\":\"بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ\"},{\"ref\":\"6:118\",\"ar\":\"فَكُلُوا۟ مِمَّا ذُكِرَ ٱسْمُ ٱللَّهِ عَلَيْهِ\"}],\"activation\":\"Both passages place ٱسْمُ beside ٱللَّهِ; 6:118 explicitly locates the mention upon what is eaten.\",\"limits\":\"The wording does not state that 1:1 is the specific formula intended in 6:118 or settle the legal application of the instruction.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-7:56\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"The passage pairs invocation in fear and hope with Allah’s mercy being near to those who do good, then depicts His mercy through wind, rain, and the revival of dead land. The focus pair’s root thus appears in both devotional and life-giving settings.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"1:1\",\"ar\":\"ٱلرَّحْمَٰنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ\"},{\"ref\":\"7:56\",\"ar\":\"وَٱدْعُوهُ خَوْفًۭا وَطَمَعًا ۚ إِنَّ رَحْمَتَ ٱللَّهِ قَرِيبٌۭ مِّنَ ٱلْمُحْسِنِينَ\"},{\"ref\":\"7:57\",\"ar\":\"بَيْنَ يَدَىْ رَحْمَتِهِۦ\"},{\"ref\":\"7:57\",\"ar\":\"فَأَنزَلْنَا بِهِ ٱلْمَآءَ\"}],\"activation\":\"Invocation and the rahma root occur together in 7:56, and 7:57 continues with rain as a form of His mercy.\",\"limits\":\"The passage does not say that the opening epithets are restricted to the good-doers named here.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-7:156\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"The verse says Allah’s mercy encompasses every thing, then says He will prescribe it for specified groups. This holds broad scope and a named distribution together, sharpening what the focus names leave unstated.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"7:156\",\"ar\":\"وَرَحْمَتِى وَسِعَتْ كُلَّ شَىْءٍۢ\"},{\"ref\":\"7:156\",\"ar\":\"فَسَأَكْتُبُهَا لِلَّذِينَ يَتَّقُونَ وَيُؤْتُونَ ٱلزَّكَوٰةَ\"}],\"activation\":\"The same verse moves from the scope of mercy to the groups for whom it will be prescribed.\",\"limits\":\"The passage does not resolve how encompassing mercy and its prescribed recipients relate in every case.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-7:180\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"Allah’s beautiful names are presented as names by which He is to be invoked; the Basmala’s Allah-name followed by Rahman and Rahim is a concrete instance of naming Him in worship.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"1:1\",\"ar\":\"بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرَّحْمَٰنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ\"},{\"ref\":\"7:180\",\"ar\":\"وَلِلَّهِ ٱلْأَسْمَآءُ ٱلْحُسْنَىٰ فَٱدْعُوهُ بِهَا\"},{\"ref\":\"7:180\",\"ar\":\"وَذَرُوا۟ ٱلَّذِينَ يُلْحِدُونَ فِىٓ أَسْمَٰٓئِهِۦ\"}],\"activation\":\"The opening formula names Allah with two epithets, while 7:180 explicitly instructs people to invoke Him by His names.\",\"limits\":\"The passage does not specifically list Rahman or Rahim among the names meant in its instruction.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-9:104\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"The verse joins accepting repentance and taking charity to the description ٱلتَّوَّابُ ٱلرَّحِيمُ. The Rahim name thus appears in a setting of communal giving and purification as well as personal return.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"9:103\",\"ar\":\"تُطَهِّرُهُمْ وَتُزَكِّيهِم بِهَا\"},{\"ref\":\"9:104\",\"ar\":\"أَنَّ ٱللَّهَ هُوَ يَقْبَلُ ٱلتَّوْبَةَ عَنْ عِبَادِهِۦ وَيَأْخُذُ ٱلصَّدَقَٰتِ\"},{\"ref\":\"9:104\",\"ar\":\"وَأَنَّ ٱللَّهَ هُوَ ٱلتَّوَّابُ ٱلرَّحِيمُ\"}],\"activation\":\"The epithet pair follows the mention of repentance and charity in the same passage.\",\"limits\":\"The wording does not identify charity as a condition for Allah’s acceptance of repentance.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-9:118\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"The three left behind reach a point where the earth and their own selves constrict them, yet they find no refuge from Allah except in Him; Allah then turns toward them and is ٱلتَّوَّابُ ٱلرَّحِيمُ. The Rahim name appears at the end of a return from acute distress.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"9:118\",\"ar\":\"وَضَاقَتْ عَلَيْهِمُ ٱلْأَرْضُ بِمَا رَحُبَتْ وَضَاقَتْ عَلَيْهِمْ أَنفُسُهُمْ\"},{\"ref\":\"9:118\",\"ar\":\"وَظَنُّوٓا۟ أَن لَّا مَلْجَأَ مِنَ ٱللَّهِ إِلَّآ إِلَيْهِ\"},{\"ref\":\"9:118\",\"ar\":\"ثُمَّ تَابَ عَلَيْهِمْ لِيَتُوبُوٓا۟ ۚ إِنَّ ٱللَّهَ هُوَ ٱلتَّوَّابُ ٱلرَّحِيمُ\"}],\"activation\":\"The verse moves from having no refuge except Allah to His turning toward them and naming Himself ٱلتَّوَّابُ ٱلرَّحِيمُ.\",\"limits\":\"This is a specific account of the three left behind, not a full definition of the focus pair.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-10:107\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"The verse places غَفُورٌ رَّحِيمٌ beside Allah’s exclusive power to remove harm and grant good, including His giving to whom He wills. The Rahim name is voiced within an account of sovereign action.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"10:107\",\"ar\":\"فَلَا كَاشِفَ لَهُۥٓ إِلَّا هُوَ\"},{\"ref\":\"10:107\",\"ar\":\"فَلَا رَآدَّ لِفَضْلِهِۦ\"},{\"ref\":\"10:107\",\"ar\":\"وَهُوَ ٱلْغَفُورُ ٱلرَّحِيمُ\"}],\"activation\":\"The verse directly precedes the epithet pair with exclusive statements about harm and good.\",\"limits\":\"It does not say that the good mentioned is identical with mercy or that mercy cancels harm.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}"}}
